artifact.self_contained_problem:
Call an n×n matrix A = (a_{ij}) over the natural numbers a magic matrix of order n if it satisfies all of the following:
(i) every entry a_{ij} belongs to {1, 2, ..., n^2};
(ii) the entries are pairwise distinct, i.e., a_{ij} ≠ a_{kl} whenever (i,j) ≠ (k,l) (so the entries are exactly a permutation of 1, 2, ..., n^2);
(iii) all n row sums, all n column sums, the sum along the main diagonal (entries a_{ii}), and the sum along the antidiagonal (entries a_{i,n+1-i}) are equal (necessarily to n(n^2+1)/2).

Let M(n) denote the number of magic matrices of order n. For general n, give either an exact formula or closed-form description of M(n), or otherwise provide non-trivial asymptotic estimates (upper and/or lower bounds) for M(n).

